            <ArticleTitle>An Evidence-Based Approach to the Treatment of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>73-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1001/jamasurg.2015.4233</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPORTANCE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is prevalent worldwide, particularly in developed countries. It is estimated that the prevalence of GERD in the United States is approximately 20% and that it is increasing because of the epidemic of obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To review the pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation, and treatment of GERD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="EVIDENCE REVIEW" NlmCategory="METHODS">A search of PubMed was conducted for the years spanning 1985 to 2015 and included the following terms: heartburn, regurgitation, dysphagia, gastroesophageal reflux disease, cough, aspiration, laryngitis, GERD, GORD, endoscopy, manometry, pH monitoring, proton pump inhibitors, open fundoplication, and laparoscopic fundoplication. Only articles in English were included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Lifestyle modifications, proton pump inhibitors, and laparoscopic fundoplication are proven treatment modalities for GERD. Endoscopic procedures have not been proven as effective. A Roux-en-Y gastric bypass is the procedure of choice when GERD and morbid obesity coexist.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS AND RELEVANCE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Gastroesophageal reflux disease is a highly prevalent disease. Once the diagnosis has been established, the best results are obtained by a multidisciplinary team with the goal of individualizing treatment for patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
